# MCP Servers Made Easy with TypeScript & OpenAI Agents

## Course Overview

This course teaches you how to build **Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers** using TypeScript while integrating them with OpenAI agents. MCP is an emerging standard for connecting language models to external tools, data sources, and services in a structured, secure way. 

**Who this course is for:**
- JavaScript/TypeScript developers looking to expand into AI/LLM integration
- Engineers building AI-powered applications or agentic systems
- Teams wanting to standardize how their LLMs interact with external services
- Developers transitioning from traditional backend work to AI-augmented platforms

**Why it matters for interview prep:**
- Demonstrates understanding of modern AI infrastructure patterns
- Shows comfort with both synchronous and asynchronous patterns in distributed systems
- Proves ability to design APIs that bridge AI models and real-world services
- Relevant for roles at companies building AI platforms, agents, and tool-use systems

---

## Core Topics Covered

- **Model Context Protocol (MCP) fundamentals** – Understanding the specification, transport mechanisms, and design philosophy
- **TypeScript for MCP servers** – Type-safe server implementation, tooling, and best practices
- **OpenAI Agent integration** – Connecting agents to MCP resources, handling tool calls, and managing context
- **Tool definition and resource management** – Designing tools with clear inputs/outputs and managing stateful resources
- **Authentication and security in MCP** – Token handling, role-based access, and secure context passing
- **Error handling and resilience patterns** – Graceful degradation, retry logic, and timeout management
- **Testing and debugging MCP servers** – Unit testing tools, integration testing agents, and observability
- **Deployment and scaling** – Running MCP servers in production, load balancing, and monitoring

---

## Learning Path Workflow

### **Phase 1: Foundation (Tasks 1–12)**
Master the basics of MCP and set up your development environment.

1. Understand the MCP specification and architecture
2. Set up TypeScript dev environment with MCP tooling
3. Create your first "Hello World" MCP server
4. Learn the request–response lifecycle
5. Understand prompts, tools, and resources in MCP
6. Parse and validate JSON-RPC messages
7. Implement basic error responses
8. Set up logging and debugging infrastructure
9. Write your first custom tool definition
10. Test a tool locally with a mock client
11. Document tool contracts using OpenAI schema format
12. Build a multi-tool MCP server

### **Phase 2: Server Implementation (Tasks 13–28)**
Develop production-grade MCP servers with proper structure.

13. Implement tool handlers with typed inputs
14. Build resource providers (files, APIs, databases)
15. Create prompt templates and context builders
16. Implement request validation and sanitization
17. Handle streaming responses from tools
18. Build stateful tool contexts
19. Implement tool chaining and composition
20. Handle timeouts and long-running operations
21. Create tool middleware and interceptors
22. Implement caching for tool responses
23. Build tool versioning and compatibility handling
24. Create configuration management for servers
25. Implement graceful shutdown and cleanup
26. Build monitoring and metrics collection
27. Implement structured logging
28. Create tool authorization and permission checks

### **Phase 3: OpenAI Agent Integration (Tasks 29–39)**
Connect your MCP servers to OpenAI agents and manage the interaction loop.

29. Set up OpenAI client with TypeScript
30. Understand function calling and tool_use patterns
31. Implement basic agent loop with tool calls
32. Map MCP tools to OpenAI function schema
33. Handle parallel tool execution
34. Implement result aggregation and feedback
35. Build context window management
36. Handle token counting and optimization
37. Implement multi-turn conversations with tools
38. Build agent memory and state management
39. Create conversation history management

### **Phase 4: Advanced Patterns (Tasks 40–48)**
Master complex scenarios and production concerns.

40. Implement recursive tool use (agents calling agents)
41. Build tool dependency graphs and validation
42. Implement fallback and recovery mechanisms
43. Create custom prompt engineering for agents
44. Build cost optimization for API calls
45. Implement rate limiting and quota management
46. Create tool performance benchmarking
47. Build distributed MCP server coordination
48. Implement secure context passing between services

### **Phase 5: Testing, Deployment & Polish (Tasks 49–54)**
Ensure reliability, observability, and production readiness.

49. Write comprehensive unit tests for tools
50. Build integration tests for agent–MCP interactions
51. Implement end-to-end testing scenarios
52. Create observability dashboards and alerts
53. Deploy MCP servers to production (cloud platforms)
54. Build monitoring, logging, and incident response

---

## Key Skills You'll Build

### **Core Technical Competencies**

| Skill | What You'll Master |
|-------|-------------------|
| **Protocol Design** | Understand request–response patterns, JSON-RPC, and async communication |
| **Type Safety** | Leverage TypeScript generics and discriminated unions for tool contracts |
| **Tool Design** | Create composable, reusable tools with clear APIs and error boundaries |
| **Agent Orchestration** | Build loops that intelligently call tools, parse responses, and adapt behavior |
| **Context Management** | Handle token windows, conversation history, and state across interactions |
| **Error Resilience** | Build graceful degradation, retry logic, and timeout patterns |
| **System Integration** | Connect LLMs to databases, APIs, files, and custom services |
| **Observability** | Instrument servers for debugging, monitoring, and production support |

### **Architectural Patterns**

- Middleware architecture for tool preprocessing
- Streaming and chunking for large responses
- Caching strategies for repeated operations
- Tool composition and dependency injection
- State machines for complex agent flows
- Circuit breakers for failing external services

---

## Sample Problem Types

### **Problem Type 1: Tool Implementation with Validation**

**Description:** Implement a tool that queries a database, with full input validation, error handling, and response formatting.

**Example:**
```typescript
// Define tool contract
const databaseQueryTool = {
  name: "query_database",
  description: "Execute a SELECT query on the analytics database",
  inputSchema: {
    type: "object" as const,
    properties: {
      query: {
        type: "string",
        description: "SQL SELECT query (whitelisted tables only)"
      },
      timeout_ms: {
        type: "number",
        description: "Query timeout in milliseconds"
      }
    },
    required: ["query"]
  }
};

// Implement handler
async function handleDatabaseQuery(params: {
  query: string;
  timeout_ms?: number;
}): Promise<{
  rows: Record<string, any>[];
  execution_time_ms: number;
  row_count: number;
}> {
  // Validate: only SELECT, no DROP/DELETE/UPDATE
  if (!/^\s*SELECT\s+/i.test(params.query)) {
    throw new Error("Only SELECT queries are allowed");
  }

  const startTime = Date.now();
  const timeout = params.timeout_ms ?? 5000;

  try {
    const rows = await executeWithTimeout(
      db.query(params.query),
      timeout
    );

    return {
      rows,
      execution_time_ms: Date.now() - startTime,
      row_count: rows.length
    };
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof TimeoutError) {
      throw new Error(`Query exceeded ${timeout}ms timeout`);
    }
    throw error;
  }
}

// Register with MCP server
server.tool("query_database", databaseQueryTool, handleDatabaseQuery);
```

**Interview angle:** Tests validation, error handling, performance awareness, and security mindset.

